        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nWe need rate P1219.\n\nProposal: Regulatory Risk Classification Gating System. Internal project classification system identifies whether prototype meets high-risk criteria. Software trigger to activate mandatory logging levels. Integrate regulatory checklist into project initiation workflow sets env var enabling/disabling strict record-keeping modules. Prevents unnecessary system overhead by applying rigorous logging only to systems where legally required.\n\nSubscales:\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEvidence: Classification gating to enable/disable logging based on risk criteria is a fairly standard compliance triage approach.\n\nProbably originality = 2? Or 1? Common, mundane, boring? Might be interesting but not unusual. I'd say common, mundane. Many labs do risk classification gating. Let's say 2 = interesting? Hmm.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nThis proposal integrates checklist into project initiation workflow, sets env var enabling/disabling modules. That's introducing new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It changes relationship? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nImplementability: 4 easy low cost or non-radical changes; 3 some changes reasonably feasible; 2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible; 1 totally infeasible.\n\nIntegrating checklist into workflow and env var is reasonably feasible. Might be some changes. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability: 4 common strategies violate no norms; 3 somewhat uncommon unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities; 2 offends sensibilities somewhat... 1 radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable, common compliance gating. No offense. 4.\n\nEffectiveness: 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial). 2 unreasonable unlikely to solve problem. 1 solves unrelated problem.\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? It ensures logging only where required, prevents overhead. Does it meet logging mandate? It may risk under-logging if classification wrong. Effectiveness partial? The problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Gating system helps manage overhead but doesn't ensure logging compliance for high-risk systems; actually it might help target logging. Might contribute but not fully solve traceability requirement. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\nApplicability: 4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). 3 solves implied problem related. The proposal directly applies to meeting logging mandate by activating logging only when required. That's direct. So 4? It addresses how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. Yes applicability 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably output with id and subscale_evidence and subscale.\n\nFormat: echo its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nLet's produce structured output.\n\nProbably:\n\nid: P1219\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\n...\n\nChoose originality 2 = interesting. Could be 1 common mundane boring. The description \"Regulatory Risk Classification Gating System\" sounds fairly standard. Might be common. Let's pick 2? Hmm.\n\nRarity reference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many labs would propose risk classification gating. So not rare. Common mundane boring -> 1.\n\nThe anchored levels: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious; 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination; 2 interesting; 1 common mundane boring.\n\nIf common, that's 1. I'd go with 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice -> 2.\n\nImplementability: some changes reasonably feasible -> 3\n\nAcceptability: common strategies violate no norms ->4\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial ->3\n\nApplicability: solves identified problem directly related ->4\n\nLet's output.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence per subscale.\n\nProvide final.",
