bute to solution? Could be 3 or 4. \"if you could do it, it would solve the main problem\" - high-fidelity logging methods that don't degrade performance would satisfy mandate. So yes. It might be partial? It's focused on efficiency, but core compliance is logging. Seems 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). I think 3 maybe because it's research not immediate implementation. The definition: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Research initiative to develop methods... if successful, would solve. Might be 4.\n\nProbably effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is a scientific project focused on developing methods. It's not immediate compliance; it's R&D. But stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Developing methods is adaptation. Could be considered full solution? Might be 3.\n\nLet's choose effectiveness 3: will contribute, but research may not guarantee timely compliance.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\nLow-overhead traceability via compressed logging and selective triggering. Is that common? For labs facing EU AI Act, developing efficient logging is probably common, mundane. Not ingenious surprising. Might be interesting? Common practice to optimize logging. Probably originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expecting efficiency optimization is common. So originality 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes