erate. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. Logging is required. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\nReasonable and will contribute to solution? It automates logging timestamps input data versioning metadata. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... Wrapper captures timestamps, input data, versioning metadata. Might capture essential events but maybe not all required specifics like reference database, identification of natural persons. It contributes to solution but partial? Could be reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, would it solve main problem? It removes manual burden and ensures consistent compliance across prototypes. Might solve logging mandate. Probably effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Or 4? Might miss specific Article 12(3) details. I'd say 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Logging mandate. So 4 = solves an identified problem directly related.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... Provide maybe lines.\n\nWill produce:\n\nid: P2476\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The wrapper decorator for automatic logging is a common observability pattern in software engineering rather than a rare or surprising invention. Score 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds a tooling layer for compliance but keeps logging as a separate operational practice rather than changing how R&D and compliance relate. Score 2.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a shared Python decorator/middleware and immutable store requires moderate engineering effort but uses familiar technologies. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automated logging to meet Article 12 is legally required and socially uncontroversial in AI labs. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It automates timestamp and input capture for prototypes, contributing to traceability though it may not fully cover Article 12(3) specifics like verifier identification. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The wrapper directly addresses the mandate to automatically record events over model lifetime for high-risk systems. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
