ill solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Level 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Does it fully solve? It ensures every system captures necessary telemetry without custom code. Might be reasonable full solution. I'd say 4? Maybe 3 because input-output pairs may not cover all required fields like reference database, natural persons. But framework can be extended.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 3? Let's think: The mandate requires logging of events relevant for identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, monitoring operation, plus specific data for certain systems. A generic middleware recording timestamps/input-output/versioning will contribute but may not fully guarantee compliance for all specific requirements (e.g., identification of natural persons). So it's partial solution.\n\nAnchored: 4 = will solve without regard workability. Could argue it would solve main problem if implemented as described. Might be reasonable to say 4. Hmm.\n\nLet's choose effectiveness 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. Evidence: It automates core telemetry but does not explicitly guarantee capture of Article 12(3) specific fields like reference database or verifier identity.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Proposal is do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes: standardized telemetry for automatic logging. That's direct. So applicability 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane.\n\nUnified compliance telemetry framework with middleware is fairly common engineering practice. Not surprising. Probably common, mundane. Maybe interesting but common. I'd rate originality 1? Or 2? Common, mundane, boring => 1. Might be interesting but