        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Need rating for proposal P1821.\n\nProposal: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline. System dynamically generates required technical documentation by extracting data directly from experiment trackers and version control. Turns raw logs into compliant reports. Integrate CI/CD triggers automatically compile dataset versions, hyperparameter logs, validation results whenever model promoted to high-risk status. Minimizes manual administrative burden.\n\nWe need subscales:\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is automated documentation pipeline extracting from trackers/version control, CI/CD triggers. This is introducing new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice? It changes relationship somewhat? It weaves logging into science via CI/CD? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nAutomated doc generation from experiment trackers is fairly common DevOps practice. Might be interesting but common. Likely 2 = interesting? Or 1 = common mundane? Probably many labs do automated reporting. I'd say 2 = interesting (or maybe 1 common). Let's pick 2.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal addresses technical documentation generation, not logging mandate per se? Article 12 is record-keeping / logging. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation and logs. Proposal focuses on technical documentation pipeline, extracting logs into compliant reports. It addresses manual burden, but logging mandate is about automatic recording of events over lifetime. The proposal turns raw logs into compliant reports, integrates CI/CD triggers compile dataset versions... It does help meet documentation requirement which is related to record-keeping. Might be partial solution: helps with documentation but not necessarily logging capabilities for traceability events per Article 12(2)(a)-(c). Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt directly relates to compliance burden. I'd say 4? Problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Documentation pipeline addresses compliance. It's directly related. Maybe 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is standard automation, acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive...\nAutomated pipeline integrating CI/CD with experiment trackers is feasible but requires some engineering. Might be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each then score.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably output list. The instruction: Return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nWe can output as lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1821\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... \nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nMake sure order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.\n\n",
