king compliance a gating factor for resources. Might be paradigm stretching? Not breaking paradigm, doesn't change what lab researches. Changes relationship. So 3.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nGateway funding tied to compliance is fairly common in regulated industries. Might be common, mundane. Probably 2? Or 1? \"Regulatory Gateway Funding Model\" maybe interesting but not rare. I'd say 2 = interesting (common enough). Could be 1 common mundane. Hmm experienced evaluator expect AI labs to tie funding to compliance milestones? Might be somewhat uncommon. Let's give 2? The anchored: 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. I think this is fairly standard project governance. Maybe 2.\n\nLet's pick originality = 2.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable...\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. This proposal ensures logging is built early, integrates compliance costs. It will contribute to meeting logging mandate by incentivizing logging, but does not itself provide technical logging solution. So partial solution? It helps ensure compliance but doesn't build logs. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. Could be 4? It would solve problem of ensuring logging is done? It ensures funding contingent on logging, but still need technical implementation. Might be partial. I'll give 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit...\nThis directly addresses how to respond to mandate by integrating compliance into resource