ght contribute partially. Effectiveness 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nAutomated documentation generator is a tool, still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new element tool but not change relationship? Might be slightly paradigm stretching = 2. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated regulatory doc generator is common, mundane. Maybe 1 or 2. Probably common practice in compliance automation. I'd say 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nLet's output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably as lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P2340\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide final.\n\n",
