vironment with synthetic risk scenarios... requires engineering effort. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4=common strategies violate no norms\n3=somewhat uncommon unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2=offends somewhat...\n1=radically violates...\n\nTesting logs is acceptable. Common? Maybe 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. It's standard validation. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree idea will solve problem.\n4=reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3=reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps partial)\n2=unreasonable unlikely...\n1=solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nSimulation framework verifies logging works before deployment. It contributes to compliance but does it solve mandate? Mandate is to have logging capabilities. Validation ensures they work, but does not itself implement logging. It's meta. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution. Not fully solve; you still need logs. Could be 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4=solves identified problem directly related\n3=solves implied problem related\n2=may have some benefit within special situation...\n1=...\n\nIt directly addresses meeting logging mandate by verifying traceability systems. That's directly related. So 4.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? Probably just list.\n\nReturn rating echoing its id exactly.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1116\noriginality_evidence: ... \noriginality: 2\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evide