emented correctly in all scenarios. Might contribute. Probably contributes to solution, partial. It checks logs meet minimum legal requirements before release. That helps ensure compliance but doesn't actually build logging. Could be partial. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but it is only a partial solution). Because need actual logging implementation first. Test suite alone won't meet mandate.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nTest suite for compliance is common QA gate. Acceptable. 4\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nBuilding automated validation tests simulating high-risk events and checking mandatory fields. That's some engineering effort but reasonable within CI/CD. Maybe 3? Could be easy? Probably requires building test harness, defining mandatory fields per Article 12. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3. Not trivial low cost? I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\nCompliance test suite for logs is common practice. Mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard complian