hes\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nUnified framework introduces new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools, formats, processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nCentralized logging library for AI compliance is fairly common/mundane. Might be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2=interesting? I'd say common. So 1.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nUnified logging framework ensures consistent data formats for traceability, automates capture. Should meet logging mandate. Solves problem of meeting logging mandate and avoid redundant spending. Seems 4 = will solve.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = ...\n\nDirectly addresses how to meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating ... give ... Probably list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2490\nacceptability_evidence: ...\naccept