ctiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (partial). Could be 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Automated pipeline does not ensure logs are recorded per Article 12(2)(a-c) specifics. It just formats docs. So partial.\n\nEvidence: The pipeline assumes compliant logs already exist and only reduces reporting burden rather than ensuring mandated event recording. Score 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Does it? Logging mandate includes documentation availability. Possibly 4? It directly addresses compliance burden. Might be 4.\n\nEvidence: Generating regulator-ready documentation from logs directly addresses the need to meet Article 12 record-keeping and Recital 71 traceability requirements. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes relationship\n4 = paradigm breaking\n\nAutomated pipeline is standard DevOps compliance automation. Probably paradigm preserving or slightly stretching. Introduces new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds automation tools to existing compliance workflow without altering how R&D relates to regulatory obligations. Score 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious.\n1 common mundane boring\nProposal is common. Automated compliance documentation pipeline is common. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: CI/CD-driven compliance reporting is a widely described industry practice and not surprising. Score 1.\n\nNeed format: return rating echoing id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON output? The instruction says give ... first write one sentence evidence... then give its score as integer level. Could be plain text.\n\nI'll output structured:\n\nid: P2955\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nMake sure order listed.\n\nLet's produce final.",
