te for compliance logs. Is it common mundane? Might be interesting. Originality 2 = interesting? Or 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. I'd say 2? Common testing practice applied to logging. Maybe 2 = interesting. Let's pick 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged? It introduces new elements (tests) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So level 2.\n\nLet's produce output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Might be simple lines.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P2698\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n\netc.\n\nMake one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure order.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Automated compliance verification testing is a standard engineering practice that does not violate legal, social, or business norms. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building synthetic failure scenarios and automated log checks requires engineering effort but uses existing test infrastructure and is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: The suite directly addresses the mandate to ensure logging meets Article 12 requirements for traceability and post-market monitoring. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It will catch non-compliant logging before deployment, contributing substantially to compliance but not itself creating the required logs. Score 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: Applying automated test suites to regulatory log capture is an interesting adaptation of common software QA practice rather than ingenious or surprising. Score 2.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It adds testing tools around logging without changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations, treating compliance as a logging practice. Scor