gies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable. Common strategies. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nCreating automated testing suite for logging is reasonably feasible, some changes. Could be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Might need synthetic test cases. Probably reasonably feasible. Maybe 3. Could be 4 easy? I'd say 3: some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal does CI gate to ensure logs meet Article12 before merge. Directly applies. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\nWill it solve? It ensures logging capabilities present. Helps prevent compliance failures. Does it fully solve mandate? Ensures logs present format accuracy but not guarantee real operation? Probably contributes. Might be partial solution. The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Gate ensures code has logging. Could be reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability? Possibly 4? It would ensure compliance at code level, preventing failures. Might be 3 = contributes to solution, partial. I think effectiveness is 3? Let's think: The problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. CI gate ensures prototypes meet standards before merge, preserving speed. That directly addresses meeting mandate. Could be 4.\n\nBut instrument says effectiveness = degree will solve problem. If you could do it, would it solve main problem? It checks logs presence format