ink applicability = 3. Evidence: It targets keeping technical documentation current, which is an implied compliance burden related to but distinct from the explicit log-recording mandate.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps, partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nIt would contribute to compliance documentation but does not address automatic event logging required by Article 12. So partial.\n\nScore 3? It helps with documentation upkeep but not logging mandate. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nAutomated documentation pipeline is common, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIntegrating MLflow and Git logs with template engine mapping metrics to EU AI Act requirements. Requires engineering effort but reasonably feasible. Might be some changes, reasonably feasible. Score 3? Could be 4 easy? Probably some changes.\n\nI'll give 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated compliance documentation pipeline is fairly common idea. Might be interesting but common. Probably 2 = interesting? Or 1 = common mundane? Many labs do auto doc generation. I'd say 2 interesting.\n\nParadigm_related