ction scripts... Is this common? Testing logging via simulation is somewhat unusual but interesting? Probably not expressed before AND ingenious? Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination -> 3. Or interesting ->2. Let's think reference class: experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give responses like implement logging, hire compliance officer, etc. Simulation suite is unusual. Maybe originality 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 4? Not expressed before AND ingenious... Probably not that surprising. I'd pick 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches =4\nparadigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...) =3\nslightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice =2\nparadigm preserving: standard compliance... unchanged =1\n\nSimulation suite introduces new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces testing environment. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost? Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Creating failure injection scripts and testing environment is significant change, expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might be 2 = significant change, expensive or difficult. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms =4. Simulation for compliance is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain text list.\n\nLet's produce structured output:\n\nid: P1254\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.\n\n",
