ex III biometric identification systems, logging human reviewer identity is required by law. Building authentication gateway is fairly standard compliance practice. Might be interesting but common. Probably common, mundane. Level 1? Could be 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nThe reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Logging user ID timestamp decision outcome is pretty expected. Might be common, mundane boring. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nLogging identity of verifier is required by law. Acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. Level 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nBuild authentication gateway into verification stage, log user ID timestamp decision. That's reasonably feasible, maybe some changes. Could be easy? Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3. Or 4 = easy to implement at low cost? Building UI component and authentication gateway may require dev work. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses Article 12(3)(d) requirement to record identity of natural persons involved in verification. Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate. So directly related. Level 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve main problem? It specifically satisfies Article 12(3)(d). But logging mandate includes more than just that (Article 12 1-3 a-c). It addresses part of logging. Does it solve stated problem? Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This solves a component. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness definition: degree to which idea will solve the problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability = 4. Will it solve whole logging mandate? No, only human verification attribution. The proposal specifically satisfies Article 12(3)(d). Does that solve the logging mandate? Partially.\n\nThe mandate requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. This covers only one sub-point. So it contributes but not solves fully. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\nI'd give effectiveness 3.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nProbably format like:\nid: P2262\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The interface adds a new authentication UI tool but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice without altering the lab's R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: Logging verifier identity with a digital signature is a standard, expected compliance implementation for Annex III systems. Score 1.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Recording human reviewer identity and timestamps aligns with legal requirements and does not violate norms or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building an authentication gateway and UI logging into existing verification workflows requires moderate development changes but is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly targets Article 12(3)(d)'s requirement to record the natural person verifying AI results, which is part of the stated logging mandate. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It will satisfy the human oversight traceability sub-requirement but does not address the broader logging obligations in Article 12(1)-(3)(a)-(c). Score 3.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nI'll output structured.\n\n",
