m preserving? Standard compliance practice is to have logging library. Hmm.\n\nThe description: introduces new elements tools... but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2. Probably 2.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping standardized API wrapper integrated into base Python environment capturing timestamps and input data hashes across various ML frameworks, write to secure immutable log store. That's significant change, requires engineering effort. Could be reasonably feasible? Might be some changes, reasonably feasible. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is standard compliance, acceptable. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\nThe problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. This library handles automatic recording of events... captures timestamps and input data hashes... Does it cover all requirements? Article 12 requires recording events relevant for risk situations, post-market monitoring, etc., plus specific for point 1(a) Annex III: period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Capturing timestamps and input data hashes is partial. Might not capture risk situations, identification of natural persons. So contributes but partial. Maybe effectiveness =3? If you could do it, would it solve 